Lady Ada Lovelace, full name Augusta Ada King, Countess of Lovelace (née Byron), was an English mathematician and writer born on 10 December 1815 and died on 27 November 1852. She was the daughter of the famous poet Lord Byron and Annabella Millbanke.

Lovelace is best known for her work on Charles Babbage's proposed mechanical general-purpose computer, the Analytical Engine. In 1843, she wrote what is considered the first published algorithm intended to be carried out by a machine, making her widely recognized as the first computer programmer.

She was a pioneer in recognizing that computers could perform tasks beyond simple calculations, such as manipulating symbols and following complex instructions. Her visionary insights laid the foundation for modern computer science.

In honor of her contributions, the Ada programming language was named after her, and Ada Lovelace Day is celebrated annually on the second Tuesday of October to recognize women's achievements in science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM).

